From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 03:36:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Blu-ray 3D: bound the base view's wait for its second view

While the MVC extension could not be read, the base view waited for it
in an unbounded queue: the picture froze and memory grew with the
bitrate. The base view now waits 4 s at most, measured as the distance
between its oldest and newest timestamps, since they can step back at a
join, and 512 packets in any case. Past that its oldest pictures are
dropped and the extension is moved to the oldest one left. The extension
goes to the play item the picture was read in, found by the position of
its bytes in what libbluray returned: it is seeked there if it is in
that play item already, else opened in it. In menu mode, where those
positions are not kept, the play item is the extension's if only its
clip holds the picture's timestamp, else the one the base view reads.
The picture's timestamp is mapped back to one of the extension's file
through the last picture read from it, which also takes the extension
past a part of its file it cannot read. With nothing read yet, the start
time the extension's timestamps are converted with is added back, unless
only the timestamp as it is falls within the play item: the conversion
leaves a timestamp below the start time as it is. The picture stays
frozen until the extension reads again; pairing then resumes with the
next base picture that has its second view. Nothing makes that a picture
the decoder can start from, so the decoder can show errors until its
next key picture, and a dependent picture cut short by a failed read can
be merged with the next one once. Passing the base view on alone would
feed the MVC decoder pictures without their second view.

Two other ways the pairing could stall for good are bounded the same
way. A dependent picture newer than every base picture threw away each
base picture as it came, without the base queue ever growing; once that
reaches a base picture 4 s past the last pair, the extension is moved to
the base view. An extension behind the base view was read up to it
whatever the distance, on the player thread, dropping each picture: past
4 s behind, or 512 pictures dropped, it is moved instead.

The pairing moves the extension at most once per 4 s of the base view,
measured on its newest picture, and a pair resets that, so an extension
that cannot be read at all is not sought on every picture. A new start
time for the extension's timestamps forgets the mapping from its last
picture to its file's timestamps, which was made with the old one.

The distance past the last pair is measured from the base view's last
pair, or from its first picture since a seek. A seamless join can move
the base view's timestamps on by any amount, so a base picture more than
4 s from the one before it starts that measure again and lifts the limit
on moves: the first pictures of the new clip are not taken to be 4 s
past a pair made in the old one. With no older base picture waiting, the
extension is then moved to that picture at once if it has ended, holds
no picture read, or holds only pictures after it, as a step back of the
timestamps leaves it, rather than after 4 s more of the base view; one
just opened in that picture's play item, with nothing read yet, is left
where it is and the pictures it had queued, all of the clip before, are
dropped. A stalled source does not move the timestamps, and starts
nothing again.

The mapping assumes one run of timestamps within a clip: in a clip with
more than one STC sequence, or one whose timestamps wrap, it can place
the extension at the wrong time, and in menu mode in the wrong clip, and
pairing waits for the next move.

Shared code, unavoidable: the 3D Blu-ray demuxer exists only in
CoreELEC's Kodi (DVDInputStreamBluray, CDemuxMVC, CDemuxStreamSSIF and
their use in DVDDemuxFFmpeg) and has no AML counterpart.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
